<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">defense</ansi>

In combat, you can attempt to defend against incoming blows in three ways: <ansi fg="command">block</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">parry</ansi>, and <ansi fg="command">dodge</ansi>.

<ansi fg="yellow">How it works:</ansi>

<ansi fg="command">Block:</ansi> If you carry a shield, you can block incoming strikes. A blocked strike does no damage and is stopped entirely. Blocking uses your shield's armor value and your Strength stat. Shield-bearers cannot dodge or parry—they can only block.

<ansi fg="command">Parry:</ansi> If you wield a melee weapon but have no shield, you can parry (turn aside) the strike with your weapon. A parried blow does no damage. Parrying uses your Speed stat and depends on your weapon type. Different weapons parry with different chances:
- Swords, staves, and polearms are good at parrying (+5%).
- Most weapons parry at baseline (axes, maces, cleaving weapons).
- Daggers parry more poorly (−5%).

<ansi fg="command">Dodge:</ansi> Anyone can dodge unless they are <ansi fg="command">stunned</ansi>. Dodging uses your Perception stat and is always available as a fallback.

<ansi fg="cyan">Who defends how?</ansi>
- Shield-bearers: block only (no dodge or parry)
- Melee weapon users (no shield): choose parry or dodge (the one with the higher chance)
- Ranged attackers: dodge only
- Unarmed fighters: dodge only
- Stunned characters: cannot defend at all

Each strike allows one defense attempt. If your defense succeeds, the blow is stopped and no armor is involved. If it fails, the strike goes through and armor comes into play as normal.

